 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS3: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Use algebraic methods to solve linear equations in one variable (including all forms that require rearrangement).",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 8 worksheets on solving equations with unknowns on both sides: negatives, brackets on both sides, fraction answers and forming equations from word problems. 100 questions with step-by-step answers.",
 "key_big": "Get the unknowns on one side first by subtracting the smaller term from both sides.",
 "key_sub": "Expand brackets first. Whatever you do to one side, do to the other.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>When the unknown is on both sides, first get all the unknowns on one side by adding or subtracting the same term from both sides. Usually remove the smaller one. Then solve as a two-step equation.</p><p>If there are brackets, expand them first. If there is a fraction, multiply both sides by the denominator.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 5<i>x</i> + 3 = 2<i>x</i> + 18.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract 2<i>x</i>: 3<i>x</i> + 3 = 18.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract 3: 3<i>x</i> = 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Divide by 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 5</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3(<i>x</i> − 2) = <i>x</i> + 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Expand: 3<i>x</i> − 6 = <i>x</i> + 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract <i>x</i>: 2<i>x</i> − 6 = 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<i>x</i> = 14.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 7</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Adding the <i>x</i> terms instead of subtracting: 5<i>x</i> + 3 = 2<i>x</i> + 18 does not become 7<i>x</i> + 3 = 18.</li><li>Moving a term without changing its sign.</li><li>Expanding only the first term in a bracket.</li><li>Dividing the wrong way round at the end: 3<i>x</i> = 15 gives <i>x</i> = 5, not 1/5.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do I solve an equation with x on both sides?",
   "a": "Subtract the smaller x term from both sides so the unknowns are on one side, then solve the two-step equation that is left."
  },
  {
   "q": "Which side should I move the x terms to?",
   "a": "Either works, but moving the smaller x term avoids negative coefficients."
  },
  {
   "q": "What if the x terms cancel out?",
   "a": "If you get a true statement like 7 = 7, every value works. If you get a false one like 5 = 2, there is no solution."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can the answer be negative or a fraction?",
   "a": "Yes. For example 5x + 1 = 2x − 7 gives x = −8/3."
  }
 ],
